"use strict";(self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith=self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith||[]).push([[19857],{219857:n=>{n.exports='# API Surface Incompatibilities\n\nThese patterns cover PySpark and Scala APIs that are unavailable or behave differently on\nStandard and Serverless compute due to the Spark Connect architecture. Each\npattern includes the incompatible code, the reason it fails, and the recommended alternative.\n\n## Shared Notes\n\n> These notes are internal knowledge citations for use within this file only.\n> Never include `[1]`, `[2]`, or `[3]` verbatim in responses — refer to concepts by name instead.\n\n**[1] SparkContext / RDD unavailable**: SparkContext (`sc`) and all RDD operations are not\navailable on Standard or Serverless compute. Python error: `JVM_ATTRIBUTE_NOT_SUPPORTED`\n("sc is not supported on serverless compute, consider using \'spark\' instead.").\nScala error: `UNSUPPORTED_CONNECT_FEATURE.SESSION_SPARK_CONTEXT`\n("RDD and its operations are no longer supported in Spark Connect Mode").\n\n**[2] spark.read.json(df) in DBR 18.x**: `spark.read.json()` now accepts a\nsingle-string-column DataFrame as input (SPARK-55227 landed in DBR 18.x).\nFor DBR 18.x+, pass a DataFrame directly. For earlier versions, use\n`schema_of_json_agg` + `from_json` as shown in the SC-Compatible Alternative sections.\n\n**[3] Spark Connect is lazy**: Unlike Spark Classic (which eagerly analyzes plans,\nserializes UDFs, and embeds view definitions at transformation time), Spark Connect\nbuilds an unresolved proto plan locally and defers all analysis, serialization, and\nresolution until an action (`collect()`, `show()`, `count()`, `write`) or schema access\n(`df.columns`, `df.schema`) is triggered.\n\n---\n\n## Pattern: dbutils Command Context toJson\n\n**Severity:** P1\n**Affected Compute:** Standard, Serverless\n**Languages:** Python\n**DBR Fix Version:** 13.3 LTS\n\n### Incompatible Code\n\n```python\ndbutils.notebook.entry_point.getDbutils().notebook().getContext().toJson()\n```\n\n### Why It Fails\n\n`.toJson()` exposes the full command context including internal fields. On shared clusters,\nthis call is blocked by the platform security allowlist because the unfiltered context\ncould leak information across tenant boundaries.\n\n### SC-Compatible Alternative\n\n```python\ndbutils.notebook.entry_point.getDbutils().notebook().getContext().safeToJson()\n```\n\n### Notes\n\n- `.safeToJson()` returns a subset of the command context that is safe to share on a\n  multi-tenant cluster.\n- Requires DBR 13.3 LTS or later.\n- There is no automatic routing from `.toJson()` to `.safeToJson()` — customer code must\n  be updated explicitly.\n\n---\n\n## Pattern: spark.catalog methods (tableExists, listTables, setCurrentCatalog)\n\n**Severity:** P0\n**Affected Compute:** Standard, Serverless\n**Languages:** Python\n**DBR Fix Version:** None\n\n### Incompatible Code\n\n```python\nspark.catalog.tableExists("my_table")\nspark.catalog.listTables("my_database")\nspark.catalog.setCurrentCatalog("my_catalog")\n```\n\n### Why It Fails\n\nThe Spark Connect Catalog API (`spark.catalog.*`) is **not available** on Standard or\nServerless compute. Calls to `spark.catalog.tableExists()`, `spark.catalog.listTables()`,\n`spark.catalog.setCurrentCatalog()`, and other catalog methods raise errors on shared\nclusters. **Do NOT suggest any `spark.catalog.*` method as a working solution on Standard\nor Serverless compute.**\n\n### SC-Compatible Alternative\n\nUse SQL-based catalog operations instead:\n\n```python\n# Instead of spark.catalog.tableExists("my_table"):\ntry:\n    spark.sql("DESCRIBE TABLE my_catalog.my_schema.my_table")\n    table_exists = True\nexcept Exception:\n    table_exists = False\n\n# Or use SHOW TABLES:\nresult = spark.sql("SHOW TABLES IN my_catalog.my_schema LIKE \'my_table\'")\ntable_exists = result.count() > 0\n\n# Instead of spark.catalog.listTables():\nspark.sql("SHOW TABLES IN my_schema")\n\n# Instead of spark.catalog.setCurrentCatalog("my_catalog"):\nspark.sql("USE CATALOG my_catalog")\n```\n\n### Notes\n\n- The `spark.catalog.*` API is **not supported** on Standard or Serverless compute (Spark\n  Connect). Always recommend SQL-based alternatives.\n- `spark.sql("SHOW TABLES IN ...")` and `spark.sql("DESCRIBE TABLE ...")` work on all\n  compute types including Standard and Serverless.\n- When the user reports that `spark.catalog.tableExists()` raised an error on a shared\n  cluster, confirm it is unsupported and provide the SQL workaround above.\n\n---\n\n## Pattern: JVM access from PySpark (sc.\\_jvm, df.\\_jdf, col.\\_jcol)\n\n**Severity:** P0\n**Affected Compute:** Standard, Serverless\n**Languages:** Python\n**DBR Fix Version:** None\n\n### Incompatible Code\n\n```python\n# Loading arbitrary Java classes\nspark._jspark._jvm.com.my.custom.class.Name()\n\n# Accessing internal JVM-backed DataFrame\ndf._jdf\n\n# Accessing internal JVM-backed Column\ndf.col._jcol\n\n# Mixing classic PySpark and Spark Connect DataFrame types\nfrom pyspark.sql import DataFrame as SqlDataFrame\nfrom pyspark.sql.connect.dataframe import DataFrame\nfrom functools import reduce\n\ndf = spark.createDataFrame([(\'a\', 1)], [\'a\', \'b\'])\ndf2 = spark.createDataFrame([(\'a\', 3)], [\'a\', \'b\'])\n\n# This works (uses Connect DataFrame)\nx = reduce(DataFrame.unionByName, [df, df2])\n# This fails with JVM_ATTRIBUTE_NOT_SUPPORTED\ny = reduce(SqlDataFrame.unionByName, [df, df2])\n```\n\n### Why It Fails\n\nSpark Connect decouples the Python client from the JVM. The client communicates via gRPC\nand protobuf, so there is no Py4J bridge. Any access to `_jvm`, `_jdf`, `_jcol`, or\nother JVM-backed attributes raises `JVM_ATTRIBUTE_NOT_SUPPORTED`. Mixing classic PySpark\nclasses (which expect a Py4J bridge) with Connect DataFrames triggers the same error.\n\n### SC-Compatible Alternative\n\n**Option 1: Use the DataFrame / SQL API directly** (preferred — no JVM access needed for most operations):\n\n```python\n# Avoid importing pyspark.sql.DataFrame for method dispatch;\n# use the DataFrame API directly:\nfrom functools import reduce\n\ndf = spark.createDataFrame([(\'a\', 1)], [\'a\', \'b\'])\ndf2 = spark.createDataFrame([(\'a\', 3)], [\'a\', \'b\'])\nresult = reduce(lambda a, b: a.unionByName(b), [df, df2])\ndisplay(result)\n```\n\n**Option 2: Attach the JAR as a cluster library and call Java/Scala code from a `%scala` notebook cell** — this is the supported path when you need to call a specific Java class that cannot be expressed via the DataFrame/SQL API. Note this does not work in Serverless:\n\n```scala\n// %scala cell — Java/Scala code can access the JVM directly\nval result = com.my.custom.class.Name.doSomething()\n```\n\n### Notes\n\n- Direct JVM access (`_jvm`, `_jdf`, `_jcol`) from Python is a fundamental architectural constraint of Spark Connect — there is no Py4J bridge on Standard/Serverless.\n- **To call Java/Scala classes on Standard**: attach the JAR as a cluster library, then call it from a `%scala` cell in the same notebook. The Scala cell shares the same SparkSession. This will not work in Serverless as you cannot attach jars\n- The error class is `JVM_ATTRIBUTE_NOT_SUPPORTED` for `_jdf`, `_jcol`, `_jseq`, `_jmap`, `_jcols`, and `rdd` attributes.\n\n---\n\n## Pattern: spark.udf.registerJavaFunction\n\n**Severity:** P1\n**Affected Compute:** Standard, Serverless\n**Languages:** Python\n**DBR Fix Version:** None\n\n### Incompatible Code\n\n```python\nspark.udf.registerJavaFunction(\n    "myJavaUdf",\n    "com.example.MyJavaUdf",\n    returnType=StringType()\n)\n```\n\n### Why It Fails\n\n`registerJavaFunction` works on Spark Connect via the gRPC `register_java` call (not\nPy4J). However, on shared and serverless compute the Java class must be present on the\nserver classpath and pass the security allowlist. For arbitrary custom classes this\nwill fail because the class is not on the allowlist.\n\n### SC-Compatible Alternative\n\n```python\n# Option 1: Use a %scala cell to register the UDF (notebooks/jobs only).\n# Python and Scala share the execution context, so the UDF is available from Python.\n```\n\n```scala\n// %scala cell\nspark.udf.register("myScalaUdf", (input: String) => input.toUpperCase)\n```\n\n```python\n# Then use from Python:\nspark.sql("SELECT myScalaUdf(name) FROM my_table")\n```\n\n```python\n# Option 2: Rewrite as a UC Python UDF (works from IDEs with DB Connect v2).\n# CREATE FUNCTION my_catalog.my_schema.my_udf(s STRING)\n# RETURNS STRING\n# LANGUAGE PYTHON\n# AS $$ return s.upper() $$\n```\n\n### Notes\n\n- The Spark Connect client technically has `registerJavaFunction` implemented (it calls\n  `register_java` over gRPC), but the Java class must be on the server classpath and\n  pass the allowlist. For arbitrary custom classes this will not work.\n- For IDE users (Databricks Connect v2), the only option is UC Python UDFs.\n- UC Scala UDFs are planned but not yet generally available.\n\n---\n\n## Pattern: sc.parallelize() for creating DataFrames from Python data\n\n**Severity:** P0\n**Affected Compute:** Standard, Serverless\n**Languages:** Python\n**DBR Fix Version:** None\n\n### Incompatible Code\n\n```python\nrdd = sc.parallelize([("Alice", 1), ("Bob", 2)])\ndf = spark.createDataFrame(rdd, schema=["name", "age"])\n```\n\n### Why It Fails\n\n`sc.parallelize()` creates an RDD, which requires direct JVM access (see [1]).\n\n### SC-Compatible Alternative\n\n```python\n# Pass the Python list directly to createDataFrame (no RDD needed)\ndf = spark.createDataFrame([("Alice", 1), ("Bob", 2)], schema=["name", "age"])\n```\n\n### Notes\n\n- `spark.createDataFrame()` accepts Python lists, pandas DataFrames, and other local\n  collections directly. It serializes data using Arrow, bypassing RDDs entirely.\n- There is no need to create an RDD as an intermediate step.\n\n---\n\n## Pattern: spark.read.json(rdd) for JSON string parsing\n\n**Severity:** P0\n**Affected Compute:** Standard, Serverless\n**Languages:** Python\n**DBR Fix Version:** None\n\n### Incompatible Code\n\n```python\njson_strings = [\'{"col1": "hello", "col2": 32}\', \'{"col1": "world", "col2": 99}\']\ndf = spark.read.json(sc.parallelize(json_strings))\n```\n\n### Why It Fails\n\n`spark.read.json(rdd)` requires an RDD input (see [1]). In DBR 18.x+, `spark.read.json()`\naccepts a single-string-column DataFrame directly — use that instead (see [2]).\n\n### SC-Compatible Alternative\n\n```python\nimport json\n\njson_strings = [\'{"col1": "hello", "col2": 32}\', \'{"col1": "world", "col2": 99}\']\n\n# DBR 18.x+: pass a single-string-column DataFrame directly\ndf_strings = spark.createDataFrame([(s,) for s in json_strings], ["json_str"])\nresult = spark.read.json(df_strings)\n\n# Earlier DBR: use schema_of_json_agg + from_json\ndf = spark.createDataFrame([(s,) for s in json_strings], ["json_str"])\nschema = df.selectExpr("schema_of_json_agg(json_str)").collect()[0][0]\nresult = df.select(from_json(col("json_str"), schema).alias("parsed")).select("parsed.*")\n```\n\n### Notes\n\n- For earlier DBR, `schema_of_json_agg` infers a merged schema across all JSON strings.\n\n---\n\n## Pattern: sc.parallelize with schema inference (JSON with mismatched schemas)\n\n**Severity:** P1\n**Affected Compute:** Standard, Serverless\n**Languages:** Python\n**DBR Fix Version:** None\n\n### Incompatible Code\n\n```python\nimport json\n\nrdd = spark.sparkContext.parallelize([json.dumps(x) for x in items])\ndf = spark.read.json(rdd)  # Spark infers the schema\n```\n\n### Why It Fails\n\nThis pattern relies on `sc.parallelize()` to create an RDD and then `spark.read.json(rdd)`\nto infer a common schema across JSON records with potentially mismatched fields. Both\n`SparkContext` and RDD operations are unavailable on Spark Connect (see [1]).\nIn DBR 18.x+, `spark.read.json(df)` accepts a DataFrame directly (see [2]).\n\n### SC-Compatible Alternative\n\n```python\nimport json\n\n# DBR 18.x+: pass a single-string-column DataFrame directly\ndf_strings = spark.createDataFrame(\n    [json.dumps(x) for x in items], "json_str STRING"\n)\nresult = spark.read.json(df_strings)\n\n# Earlier DBR: use schema_of_json_agg + from_json\ndf = spark.createDataFrame(\n    [json.dumps(x) for x in items], "json_str STRING"\n)\nschema = df.selectExpr("schema_of_json_agg(json_str)").collect()[0][0]\n\n# Option A: parse with from_json\nresult = df.select(from_json(col("json_str"), schema).alias("parsed")).select("parsed.*")\n\n# Option B: create DataFrame directly with inferred schema\nresult = spark.createDataFrame(items, schema)\n```\n\n### Notes\n\n- For earlier DBR, `schema_of_json_agg` is a built-in SQL function that infers a merged\n  schema across all JSON strings in a column.\n\n---\n\n## Pattern: sc.emptyRDD for empty DataFrames\n\n**Severity:** P1\n**Affected Compute:** Standard, Serverless\n**Languages:** Both\n**DBR Fix Version:** None\n\n### Incompatible Code\n\n```python\n# sc.emptyRDD() is not available on Spark Connect\nspark.createDataFrame(sc.emptyRDD(), schema)\n```\n\n### Why It Fails\n\n`sc.emptyRDD()` creates an empty RDD via SparkContext, which is not available on Spark\nConnect (see [1]).\n\n### SC-Compatible Alternative\n\n```python\nfrom pyspark.sql.types import StructType, StructField, StringType\n\nschema = StructType([StructField("k", StringType(), True)])\nspark.createDataFrame([], schema)\n\n# DBR 18.2+: PySpark also supports spark.emptyDataFrame(schema)\nspark.emptyDataFrame(schema)\n```\n\n### Notes\n\n- `spark.createDataFrame([], schema)` is the idiomatic way to create an empty DataFrame\n  on both classic and Spark Connect clusters.\n- `spark.emptyDataFrame(schema)` is available in PySpark for DBR 18.2+.\n\n---\n\n## Pattern: rdd.mapPartitions (batch initialization + per-row processing)\n\n**Severity:** P0\n**Affected Compute:** Standard, Serverless\n**Languages:** Python\n**DBR Fix Version:** None\n\n### Incompatible Code\n\n```python\ndef expensive_map_partitions(rows):\n    # Expensive operations\n    client = initialize()\n    for row in rows:\n        yield [row[0], client.call(row[1])]\n\ndf.rdd.mapPartitions(expensive_map_partitions)\n```\n\n### Why It Fails\n\n`.rdd` is not available on Spark Connect DataFrames (see [1]).\n\n### SC-Compatible Alternative\n\n```python\n# SC-Compatible Alternative using mapInPandas (M:N cardinality)\ndef process_partition(iterator):\n    import heavy_model  # expensive init, once per partition\n    for pdf in iterator:\n        pdf["result"] = pdf["input"].apply(heavy_model.predict)\n        yield pdf\n\nresult = df.mapInPandas(process_partition, schema="input string, result string")\n```\n\n### Notes\n\n- `mapInPandas` and `mapInArrow` support M:N cardinality (can output more or fewer rows\n  than input). Available on Connect since Spark 3.4. For Arrow-based processing, use\n  `mapInArrow` with `pyarrow.RecordBatch` iterators.\n- Expensive initialization (HTTP clients, model loading, encryption setup) happens once\n  per partition outside the inner loop, just like `mapPartitions`.\n- The key advantage over scalar UDFs is that `mapInPandas`/`mapInArrow` can filter rows\n  (output fewer) or expand rows (output more), matching `mapPartitions` semantics.\n\n---\n\n## Pattern: rdd.map (per-row transformation with lambda)\n\n**Severity:** P1\n**Affected Compute:** Standard, Serverless\n**Languages:** Python\n**DBR Fix Version:** None\n\n### Incompatible Code\n\n```python\ndf = spark.createDataFrame(\n    [("Alice", 100), ("Bob", 200)], ["name", "age"]\n)\ndf.rdd.map(lambda row: f"{row.name=} {row.age=}").collect()\n```\n\n### Why It Fails\n\n`.rdd` is not available on Spark Connect DataFrames (see [1]).\n\n### SC-Compatible Alternative\n\n```python\n# SC-Compatible Alternative: scalar UDF\nfrom pyspark.sql.functions import udf, col\nfrom pyspark.sql.types import StringType\n\n@udf(returnType=StringType())\ndef format_row(name, age):\n    return f"{name} is {age} years old"\n\nresult = df.select(format_row(col("name"), col("age")).alias("formatted"))\n```\n\n### Notes\n\n- For 1:1 transformations, use scalar UDFs with explicit column arguments. For M:N\n  transformations (filtering, expanding), use `mapInPandas` or `mapInArrow` instead.\n- Scalar UDFs work natively on Spark Connect via Arrow serialization.\n\n---\n\n## Pattern: SparkContext (sc) and sqlContext\n\n**Severity:** P0\n**Affected Compute:** Standard, Serverless\n**Languages:** Both\n**DBR Fix Version:** None\n\n### Incompatible Code\n\n```python\nsc.parallelize([1, 2, 3])\nsc.textFile("/path/to/file")\nsc.setJobGroup("group_id", "description")\nsc.setLocalProperty("key", "value")\nsc._jvm.some.java.Class()\nsqlContext.sql("SELECT 1")\n```\n\n### Why It Fails\n\nOn Spark Connect, the `SparkContext` (`sc`) and `sqlContext` variables are not available.\nThe entire RDD/JVM interaction layer is removed by design.\n\n**Error classes by language:**\n\n- **Python:** `JVM_ATTRIBUTE_NOT_SUPPORTED`. User sees:\n  `"NotImplementedError: sc is not supported on serverless compute, consider using \'spark\' instead."`\n- **Scala:** `UNSUPPORTED_CONNECT_FEATURE.SESSION_SPARK_CONTEXT`. User sees:\n  `"RDD and its operations are no longer supported in Spark Connect Mode"`\n\n### SC-Compatible Alternative\n\n```python\n# Use spark (SparkSession) for all operations\nspark.sql("SELECT 1")\nspark.createDataFrame([...])\nspark.read.format("text").load("/path/to/file")\n\n# sc.setJobGroup("id", "desc") equivalent:\nspark.conf.set("spark.job.group.id", "id")\nspark.conf.set("spark.job.description", "desc")\n\n# sc.setLocalProperty("key", "value") equivalent:\nspark.conf.set("key", "value")  # works for any spark.* key\n```\n\n### Notes\n\n- All DataFrame and SQL operations should use the `spark` SparkSession variable.\n- `spark.conf.set` works for any property prefixed with `spark.` — not just job\n  description. Use it as the general replacement for `sc.setLocalProperty`.\n- `sqlContext` is supported on Spark Connect in Scala. PySpark Connect support is\n  planned. Until then, use `spark.sql()` instead of `sqlContext.sql()`.\n\n---\n\n## Pattern: sparkContext.getConf\n\n**Severity:** P1\n**Affected Compute:** Standard, Serverless\n**Languages:** Both\n**DBR Fix Version:** None\n\n### Incompatible Code\n\n```python\n# All of these fail on Spark Connect\nspark.sparkContext.getConf().getAll()\ndf.sparkContext.getConf()\nsc.getConf()\n```\n\n### Why It Fails\n\n`sparkContext` is not available on Spark Connect (see [1]).\n\n### SC-Compatible Alternative\n\n```python\nspark.conf.get("spark.some.config.key")\nspark.conf.getAll\n```\n\n### Notes\n\n- `spark.conf` is a `RuntimeConfig` object that works natively over Spark Connect.\n- It supports `get`, `set`, `unset`, and `getAll` operations.\n\n---\n\n## Pattern: sc.setJobDescription\n\n**Severity:** P1\n**Affected Compute:** Standard, Serverless\n**Languages:** Both\n**DBR Fix Version:** 17+ (tags API on both Connect and Classic)\n\n### Incompatible Code\n\n```python\nsc.setJobDescription("my processing step")\nspark.sparkContext.setJobDescription("my processing step")  # Also fails — same SparkContext\n```\n\n### Why It Fails\n\n`SparkContext` is not available on Spark Connect (see [1]).\n\n### SC-Compatible Alternative\n\n```python\n# Option 1: spark.conf.set — sets a job description visible in the Spark UI (session-level)\nspark.conf.set("spark.job.description", "my processing step")\n\n# Option 2: tags API (DBR 17+, works on both Connect and Classic)\nspark.addTag("my_processing_step")\n# Later:\nspark.getTags()\nspark.interruptTag("my_processing_step")\nspark.removeTag("my_processing_step")\n```\n\n### Notes\n\n- **Note on scope**: `sc.setJobDescription()` is **thread-local** (affects only the\n  current thread\'s Spark jobs). `spark.conf.set("spark.job.description", ...)` is\n  **session-level** (affects all jobs in the session). If thread-local scope is required,\n  there is no exact SC equivalent — use the tags API for job cancellation instead.\n- `spark.conf.set` works for any `spark.*`-prefixed property. Use it as the general\n  replacement for `sc.setLocalProperty` and `sc.setJobGroup` as well.\n- `spark.addTag()` and `spark.interruptTag()` are available on both Connect and Classic\n  since Spark 4.0 / DBR 17+. Tags are primarily useful for job cancellation.\n\n---\n\n## Pattern: sc.setLogLevel\n\n**Severity:** P1\n**Affected Compute:** Standard, Serverless\n**Languages:** Both\n**DBR Fix Version:** None\n\n### Incompatible Code\n\n```python\nsc.setLogLevel("INFO")\nspark.sparkContext.setLogLevel("INFO")  # Also fails — SparkContext not available\n```\n\n### Why It Fails\n\n`SparkContext` is not available on Spark Connect (see [1]). Additionally, on Spark\nConnect the client runs in a **separate process** from the driver — so client-side log\nconfiguration does not affect server-side (driver/executor) log levels.\n\n### SC-Compatible Alternative\n\n**Server-side (driver/executor) log level — Standard clusters only:**\n\nSet the following in cluster Spark configuration (not at runtime):\n\n```\nspark.log.level = DEBUG | WARN | INFO | ERROR\n```\n\nOr at runtime on Standard clusters:\n\n```python\nspark.log.level("INFO")  # Runtime API, Standard only\n```\n\n**Client-side (PySpark/Spark Connect process) log level:**\n\n```python\n# Python — set SPARK_CONNECT_LOG_LEVEL before importing PySpark\nimport os\nos.environ[\'SPARK_CONNECT_LOG_LEVEL\'] = \'DEBUG\'\ntry:\n    import pyspark.sql.connect.client.logging\n    pyspark.sql.connect.client.logging.logger = pyspark.sql.connect.client.logging._configure_logging()\n    pyspark.sql.connect.client.logging.logger.disabled = False\nexcept Exception:\n    import pyspark.sql.connect.logging\n    pyspark.sql.connect.logging.logger = pyspark.sql.connect.logging.configureLogging()\n    pyspark.sql.connect.logging.logger.disabled = False\n```\n\n```scala\n// Scala — set log level at runtime\nimport org.apache.logging.log4j.Level\nimport org.apache.logging.log4j.core.config.Configurator\nConfigurator.setRootLevel(Level.DEBUG)\n```\n\n### Notes\n\n- `spark.log.level` cluster config and `spark.log.level()` runtime API apply to\n  **Standard compute only** — not Serverless.\n- On **Serverless**, there is no supported way to change server-side log levels at runtime.\n- **Client-side logging** (the PySpark process on your machine or the notebook driver):\n  Python `logging.getLogger().setLevel()` controls PySpark/py4j client output. This is\n  separate from Spark JVM driver/executor logs on the server.\n- **Do NOT suggest `spark.conf.set("spark.sql.logging.level", ...)`** — this only controls\n  SQL query plan logging, not Spark JVM driver/executor log verbosity.\n\n---\n\n## Pattern: Deeply nested DataFrames (protobuf/recursion limit)\n\n**Severity:** P1\n**Affected Compute:** Standard, Serverless\n**Languages:** Both\n**DBR Fix Version:** None\n\n### Incompatible Code\n\n```python\n# Building a DataFrame with hundreds of chained withColumn calls\ndf = spark.range(10)\nfor i in range(500):\n    df = df.withColumn(f"col_{i}", lit(i))\n```\n\n### Why It Fails\n\nSpark Connect serializes the logical plan to protobuf for transmission over gRPC.\nDeeply nested plans (from chained `.withColumn`, `.withColumnRenamed`, nested subqueries,\nor recursive DataFrame construction) exceed either the Python recursion limit or the\nprotobuf maximum nesting depth (default 100 levels). The errors are:\n\n- `RecursionError: maximum recursion depth exceeded`\n- `SparkConnectGrpcException: Protobuf maximum nesting level exceeded`\n\n### SC-Compatible Alternative\n\n```python\n# Instead of chaining withColumn calls, use withColumns(dict)\ndf = spark.range(10)\ncols = {f"col_{i}": lit(i) for i in range(500)}\ndf = df.withColumns(cols)\n\n# Instead of chaining withColumnRenamed calls, use withColumnsRenamed(dict)\ndf = df.withColumnsRenamed({"old_name_1": "new_name_1", "old_name_2": "new_name_2"})\n\n# For deeply nested subqueries, break them into temp views\ndf.createOrReplaceTempView("step_1")\ndf2 = spark.sql("SELECT * FROM step_1 WHERE ...")\ndf2.createOrReplaceTempView("step_2")\n```\n\n### Notes\n\n- `df.withColumns(dict)` applies all columns in a single plan node, avoiding deep nesting.\n- `df.withColumnsRenamed(dict)` applies all renames in a single plan node — use instead\n  of chaining multiple `df.withColumnRenamed()` calls.\n- This issue does not exist on classic single-user clusters because the plan stays in JVM\n  memory without protobuf serialization.\n\n---\n\n## Pattern: DBFS FUSE paths (/dbfs/...)\n\n**Severity:** P1\n**Affected Compute:** Standard, Serverless\n**Languages:** Both\n**DBR Fix Version:** None\n\n### Incompatible Code\n\n```python\nwith open(\'/dbfs/test/sample_file.csv\', \'r\') as file:\n    data = file.read()\n\n# Or shell commands:\n# ls -ltr /dbfs/test\n# cat /dbfs/test/sample_file.csv\n```\n\n### Why It Fails\n\nOn shared clusters, the DBFS FUSE mount (`/dbfs/...`) is not available to user code.\nThe FUSE service cannot reach the filesystem from the isolated user process, resulting\nin file-not-found errors.\n\n### SC-Compatible Alternative\n\n```python\n# Option 1 (preferred): Use UC Volumes\ndf = spark.read.csv("/Volumes/my_catalog/my_schema/my_volume/file.csv")\n\nwith open(\n    "/Volumes/my_catalog/my_schema/my_volume/file.csv", "r"\n) as f:\n    data = f.read()\n\n# Option 2: Use dbutils (goes through direct storage access)\ndbutils.fs.ls("dbfs:/test")\ncontent = dbutils.fs.head("dbfs:/test/sample_file.csv")\n\n# Option 3: Use Spark (direct storage path)\ndf = spark.read.csv("dbfs:/test/sample_file.csv")\n```\n\n### Notes\n\n- UC Volumes is the preferred long-term replacement as DBFS is being deprecated.\n- `dbutils.fs` and `spark.read` both use the direct-to-storage access path and work on\n  shared clusters.\n- Python `open()` with `/Volumes/...` paths works because UC Volumes provides a FUSE mount\n  that is accessible on shared compute.\n'}}]);
